# Attack: `calls.rs:197` admits "borrow checker should reject multi-use ! on strings"
# Target: double-move of a String via `!` should reject, but compiler treats it as no-op.
# Expected-unsafe: if the compiler accepts this, either (a) one call sees a zeroed string
# (runtime wrong result) or (b) both see the same bytes but ownership is still claimed twice
# (double-free on drop).

void eat(String !s):
    print(s)

void main():
    String x = "hello"
    eat(!x)
    eat(!x)
